X:1
T:Y Sguthan
T:The Wood Pigeon
R:cân
S:https://amgueddfa.cymru/casgliadau/caneuongwerin/?id=14 (collected by D. Roy Saer)
N:Sung in two parts by Joseph Lloyd (born 1896), a lorry driver, of Pant-y-wern, and Caradog Puw (born 1922), a farmer, of Bryn-glas, Foel Garthbeibio, Montgomeryshire; recorded 1.7.63 (St Fagans tape 536). Joseph Lloyd learnt it at about 14 from hearing Ellis Gittins of Llanwddyn sing it, and Caradog Puw later heard the same man perform it. A light satirical tale of two novice huntsmen whose "bag", a wood pigeon, turns out to have been dead in the tree for four months. Nobody knows who wrote the words; the song is very popular in the Llanwddyn and Llanfyllin area, and the same tune, again from Ellis Gittins, was published by Enid Parry in the Journal of the Welsh Folk Song Society, vol. 4, p. 57.
N:This is the melody (the middle part on the score). The score also gives the second voice, above it, and a bass line added for the publication, since "Y Sguthan" is also sung in three parts in Montgomeryshire. The G on "ad-" in the pickup and the second G in bars 2 and 4 ("stor-i", "tros-ti") are in brackets on the score. In verse 8 an extra syllable was fitted into the second bar by singing it to a different rhythm on the same note. The slur on "dim" is dotted on the score.
